  "knowledgePattern": "Compare rational and exact trigonometric values without decimal approximation",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Compare the fractions exactly",
      "body": "<p>Use the exact cosine value to simplify the trigonometric option. Compare two fractions at a time by cross-multiplying, keeping the larger one for the next comparison.</p>",
      "recap": "With positive denominators, cross-products compare two fractions without rounding.",
      "pitfall": "Start each comparison from the original fractions to keep the numbers small.",
      "pause": "Find the largest of the four fractions.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l04",
          "reminder": "Check the sign of a multiplier before multiplying an inequality by it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Remove the square root by squaring",
      "body": "<p>Check that the remaining fraction and surd are both positive. You can then compare their squares.</p>",
      "recap": "Squaring preserves the order of positive numbers.",
      "pitfall": "This rule would not compare a negative number with a positive one in the same way.",
      "pause": "Compare the two squares exactly and choose the larger original value.",
      "recall": []
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The trigonometric value is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>. Compare the rational candidates using positive cross-products: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>49</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>120</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>119</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>17</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>80</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>77</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> exceeds the other three fractions.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Both remaining quantities are positive. Squaring gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>7</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>49</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>98</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>49</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>. Answer C.</p>"
